          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid retrieval and rank fusion strategies.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - pattern/Hybrid-Search\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two sources: initial stub on rank fusion \\u002B new source on hybrid retrieval combining sparse and dense methods.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets. This dual-method approach captures both exact term matches and semantic similarity, addressing complementary strengths:\\n\\n- **Sparse lexical search** (e.g. BM25) excels at exact term matching, particularly useful for rare identifiers like error codes or product SKUs.\\n- **Dense vector search** captures semantic meaning and conceptual similarity across natural language.\\n\\n## Rank Fusion\\n\\nResults from multiple retrieval methods are merged, often via **reciprocal rank fusion**, to produce a unified ranked set. This approach has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, consistently outperforming either method alone on queries that mix rare identifiers with natural language intent.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval source (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-10)\\n\u0022}"
